BT /F2 11 Tf 0.12 0.22 0.4 rg 50 245.0 Td (3.1 Definition) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 229.0 Td (Clean code is code that a professional developer can read and understand within minutes,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 217.5 Td (without explanation. It is not about clever tricks or terseness - it is about clarity, honesty,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 206.0 Td (and precision.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 190.5 Td (Robert C. Martin's definition: *"Clean code reads like well-written prose."*) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 0.12 0.22 0.4 rg 50 165.0 Td (3.2 Historical Reason) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 149.0 Td (In the 1970s-1980s, code was written to run efficiently on constrained hardware. Variable names) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 137.5 Td (were `i`, `j`, `x`, `tmp` because memory was expensive. By the 1990s, hardware was fast enough) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 126.0 Td (that human reading time became the bottleneck - not machine execution. The industry realized) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 114.5 Td (that code is read 10× more often than it is written, and the cost of maintaining unclear code) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 103.0 Td (far exceeds any brevity benefit.) Tj ET
