BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 790.0 Td (A: "What problem does this solve, and how will we know if it's solved?" Requirements are often) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 778.5 Td (solutions disguised as problems. Understanding the underlying need prevents building the wrong) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 767.0 Td (thing perfectly.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 751.5 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 0.12 0.22 0.4 rg 50 726.0 Td (SOLID - Interview Tricky Questions) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 710.0 Td (**Tricky Q:** "Is it SOLID if you put everything in one big interface but inherit from it) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 698.5 Td (everywhere?") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 683.0 Td (A: No - this violates ISP. A class implementing a fat interface is forced to depend on methods) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 671.5 Td (it doesn't use, creating tight coupling and fragility.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 656.0 Td (**Tricky Q:** "Can you violate SOLID intentionally?") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 640.5 Td (A: Yes, and experienced engineers do. SOLID principles are heuristics, not laws. For a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 629.0 Td (time-boxed proof-of-concept, for a codebase being deprecated in 6 months, or for a truly simple) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 617.5 Td (domain with no expected change - over-engineering to SOLID adds cost with no benefit. The key) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 606.0 Td (is: document the violation, its reason, and the plan to address it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 590.5 Td (**Tricky Q:** "Does Dependency Injection implement the Dependency Inversion Principle?") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 575.0 Td (A: DI is a *mechanism*. DIP is a *principle*. You can use DI without following DIP \(inject) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 563.5 Td (concrete classes instead of abstractions\). DIP is achieved when you inject *abstractions*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 552.0 Td (\(interfaces\), and the infrastructure implements those abstractions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 536.5 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 0.12 0.22 0.4 rg 50 511.0 Td (Architecture - Interview Questions) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 495.0 Td (**Basic:**) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 479.5 Td (Q: What is the difference between monolith and microservices?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 464.0 Td (A: A monolith is a single deployable unit; microservices are independent deployable services.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 452.5 Td (Monoliths excel in simplicity and consistency; microservices in independent scaling and team) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 441.0 Td (autonomy. The right choice depends on team size, traffic patterns, and organizational maturity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 425.5 Td (**Senior Follow-up:**) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 410.0 Td (Q: When would you recommend staying with a monolith despite high traffic?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 394.5 Td (A: High traffic alone doesn't justify microservices. I'd recommend staying monolithic if: \(1\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 383.0 Td (the team is small and can't manage the operational overhead, \(2\) the domain is stable with few) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 371.5 Td (distinct scaling axes, \(3\) data consistency requirements are strong \(many cross-entity) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 360.0 Td (transactions\). I'd extract services surgically only where the monolith creates actual scaling or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 348.5 Td (autonomy pain.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 333.0 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 0.12 0.22 0.4 rg 50 307.5 Td (Clean Code - Interview Tricky Questions) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 291.5 Td (**Tricky Q:** "Is commented-out code acceptable in production?") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 276.0 Td (A: No. Version control \(git\) exists precisely to preserve history. Commented-out code adds) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 264.5 Td (noise, confuses readers, and is never cleaned up. Delete it; git history preserves it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 249.0 Td (**Tricky Q:** "What's wrong with a method named `ProcessData\(\)`?") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 233.5 Td (A: It reveals nothing about what the method does. Who calls it? What data? What happens to it?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 222.0 Td (Meaningful names are `ProcessIncomingPaymentWebhook\(\)` or `TransformHealthclaimToFhirFormat\(\)`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 210.5 Td (Vague names like `Process`, `Handle`, `Manage`, and `Do` are red flags for methods that do too) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 199.0 Td (much.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 183.5 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 168.0 Td (*End of Volume 0: Software Engineering Fundamentals*) Tj ET
