BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 177.0 Td (11. Production Problems: Defensive Copies Causing CPU Spikes in Microservices) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 163.0 Td (**Incident**: A high-frequency telemetry analytics microservice began experiencing 98% CPU) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 151.5 Td (saturation after refactoring to use `in` parameters for performance optimization.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 136.0 Td (**Root Cause**: The engineering team changed all methods to accept `in TelemetryReading) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 124.5 Td (reading` where `TelemetryReading` was a 96-byte struct. However, `TelemetryReading` was declared) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 113.0 Td (as a regular `struct` with 12 getter properties. Every property access inside processing loops) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 101.5 Td (triggered a compiler-generated defensive copy. In a loop of 10,000 items, the code created) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 90.0 Td (120,000 defensive copies on the stack frame, completely saturating CPU L1 cache bandwidth.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 74.5 Td (**Post-Mortem Resolution**: Marked `TelemetryReading` as a `public readonly struct`. Defensive) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 63.0 Td (copies dropped to zero, and CPU consumption dropped from 98% to 11%.) Tj ET
