BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 776.0 Td (**Basic**: "What is the difference between `ref` and `out` parameters?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 765.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 763.0 Td (*Answer*: Both pass arguments by reference via managed pointers. A `ref` parameter) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 752.0 Td (requires the variable to be initialized by the caller before being passed into the method.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 741.0 Td (An `out` parameter does not require caller initialization, but the callee is required by) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 730.0 Td (the compiler to assign a value to it on all code paths before returning.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 719.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 717.0 Td (**Intermediate**: "What is the 'defensive copy' problem with `in` parameters, and how do) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 706.0 Td (you resolve it?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 695.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 693.0 Td (*Answer*: The `in` parameter passes a struct by read-only reference. If the struct is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 682.0 Td (mutable \(not declared as `readonly struct`\), the C# compiler cannot verify that calling a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 671.0 Td (property or method on that struct will not modify its fields. To guarantee immutability,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 660.0 Td (the compiler emits IL that copies the struct onto a local stack variable before every) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 649.0 Td (member invocation. This defeats the performance goal of `in`. The fix is to always declare) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 638.0 Td (the struct as `public readonly struct`.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 627.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 625.0 Td (**Senior**: "What are `ref return` and `ref readonly return`? In what architecture would) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 614.0 Td (you use them?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 603.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 601.0 Td (*Answer*: `ref return` allows a method to return a direct alias to an internal storage) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 590.0 Td (location \(such as an array slot or struct field\) rather than copying its value. The caller) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 579.0 Td (can modify the target directly in place. `ref readonly return` returns the reference) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 568.0 Td (without permitting mutation. It is used in high-throughput architectures like game) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 557.0 Td (engines, audio processors, and financial matrix buffers where massive value types must be) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 546.0 Td (read continuously with zero copying and zero GC allocation.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 535.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 533.0 Td (**Tricky**: "Why does struct field ordering matter for performance and memory footprint?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 522.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 520.0 Td (*Answer*: The CLR aligns primitive fields to memory boundaries matching their size \(e.g.,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 509.0 Td (an 8-byte `long` must be aligned on an address divisible by 8\). If an alternating pattern) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 498.0 Td (of 1-byte `byte` and 8-byte `long` fields is defined, the runtime injects 7 bytes of dead) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 487.0 Td (padding after each byte. Reordering fields from largest to smallest eliminates padding,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 476.0 Td (reduces the struct's cache footprint, and prevents cache-line bouncing across multi-core) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 465.0 Td (architectures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 452.0 Td (---) Tj ET
