BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 633.5 Td (1. Definition) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 619.5 Td (Concurrent collections \(`System.Collections.Concurrent`\) are high-performance, thread-safe data) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 608.0 Td (structures designed specifically for multi-threaded and parallel access. They eliminate coarse) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 596.5 Td (global locking by utilizing fine-grained locking \(lock striping\), lock-free atomic CAS) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 585.0 Td (\(`Interlocked`\) operations, and segmented circular arrays to maximize concurrent throughput.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 569.5 Td (2. Historical Reason) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 555.5 Td (Prior to .NET 4.0, developers relied on standard collections \(`List<T>`, `Dictionary<TKey,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 544.0 Td (TValue>`\) wrapped in global `lock` blocks or `Hashtable.Synchronized\(\)`. Under high concurrency) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 532.5 Td (across multiple CPU cores, a single global lock creates severe lock contention: all threads) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 521.0 Td (serialize behind a single monitor, causing severe CPU cache thrashing and negating the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 509.5 Td (advantages of multi-core hardware. Furthermore, using a standard `Dictionary` concurrently) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 498.0 Td (without locks corrupts internal hash buckets and can trap reader threads in **infinite loops**) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 486.5 Td (during internal table resizing.) Tj ET
