BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 370.5 Td (**Basic**: "How does `ConcurrentDictionary` achieve thread safety without locking on) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 359.5 Td (reads?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 348.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 346.5 Td (*Answer*: `ConcurrentDictionary` uses lock-free volatile reads and memory barriers for) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 335.5 Td (its read operations \(`TryGetValue`, indexer\). Readers read bucket pointers directly from) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 324.5 Td (the underlying array without acquiring locks. Writes use fine-grained lock striping,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 313.5 Td (locking only the specific shard bucket being modified.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 302.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 300.5 Td (**Intermediate**: "Why can the factory delegate in `ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd\(\)`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 289.5 Td (execute multiple times for the same key?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 278.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 276.5 Td (*Answer*: To prevent performance degradation, the CLR executes the factory delegate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 265.5 Td (outside of the striped bucket lock. If multiple threads request the same missing key) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 254.5 Td (simultaneously, each thread executes the delegate concurrently. The dictionary then uses) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 243.5 Td (an atomic operation to insert only the first completed result, discarding the others.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 232.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 230.5 Td (**Senior**: "How do you guarantee that a factory delegate in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 219.5 Td (`ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd\(\)` is executed exactly once?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 208.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 206.5 Td (*Answer*: Wrap the value in a `Lazy<Task<T>>`:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 193.5 Td (`dict.GetOrAdd\(key, k => new Lazy<Task<T>>\(\(\) => ExpensiveCallAsync\(k\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 182.0 Td (LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication\)\)`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 166.5 Td (The dictionary atomically inserts the `Lazy` instance. Awaiting `lazy.Value` guarantees that) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 155.0 Td (the underlying asynchronous workload executes strictly once, regardless of thread contention.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 141.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 139.5 Td (**Tricky**: "What is the internal design of `ConcurrentQueue<T>` and how does it avoid) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 128.5 Td (thread contention?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 117.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 115.5 Td (*Answer*: `ConcurrentQueue<T>` is implemented as a singly-linked list of 32-element array) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 104.5 Td (segments. Each segment acts as a circular ring buffer with local Head and Tail pointers.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 93.5 Td (Enqueue and dequeue operations update these pointers using `Interlocked.CompareExchange`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 82.5 Td (without acquiring locks. When a segment is filled, a new segment is atomically linked to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 71.5 Td (the chain.) Tj ET
