0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 253.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 251.0 Td (*Answer*: When both are `true`, the CLR skips all multi-producer/multi-consumer) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 240.0 Td (synchronization logic \(like internal locks or CAS loops\). It instantiates an optimized,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 229.0 Td (lock-free, single-reader single-writer circular ring buffer that uses simple volatile) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 218.0 Td (pointer updates, maximizing CPU cache locality and delivering raw throughput exceeding 20) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 207.0 Td (million operations per second.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 196.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 194.0 Td (**Tricky**: "Why should you prefer `System.Threading.Channels` over) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 183.0 Td (`BlockingCollection<T>` in modern ASP.NET Core applications?") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 172.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 170.0 Td (*Answer*: `BlockingCollection<T>` relies on synchronous, blocking thread synchronization) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 159.0 Td (\(`Take\(\)` blocks the calling thread\). In ASP.NET Core, blocking worker threads causes) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 148.0 Td (ThreadPool starvation and degrades throughput. `Channels` provides non-blocking) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 137.0 Td (asynchronous APIs \(`ReadAsync`, `WriteAsync`\) returning `ValueTask`, allowing threads to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 126.0 Td (return to the pool while waiting for data, maintaining high scalability.) Tj ET
