BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 744.0 Td (**Tricky**: "Explain the internal differences between Range Partitioning and Chunk) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 733.0 Td (Partitioning in PLINQ.") Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 722.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 720.0 Td (*Answer*: Range Partitioning is used when the data source implements `IList<T>` \(known) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 709.0 Td (length and random access\). PLINQ divides the total count into equal static index ranges) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 698.0 Td (per worker thread, eliminating synchronization overhead during iteration. Chunk) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 687.0 Td (Partitioning is used for generic `IEnumerable<T>` sources where the count is unknown;) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 676.0 Td (threads dynamically take small chunks of items from a shared enumerator using) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 665.0 Td (synchronization. Chunk partitioning introduces slight synchronization overhead but) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 654.0 Td (provides superior load balancing if processing time per item is unpredictable.) Tj ET
