BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 790.0 Td (**Intermediate:** "What's the difference between `override` and `new` in C#?") Tj ET
0.94 0.96 0.99 rg 50 716.5 495 58 re f
0.15 0.45 0.85 rg 50 716.5 4 58 re f
0.85 0.88 0.92 RG 0.5 w 50 716.5 495 58 re s
BT /F2 8 Tf 0.15 0.45 0.85 rg 62 764.5 Td ([NOTE]) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 764.5 Td (      `override` participates in virtual dispatch - when called through a base class) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 753.5 Td (reference, the derived class's method runs. `new` hides the base method without) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 742.5 Td (replacing it in the vtable, so calling through a base class reference still calls the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 731.5 Td (base method. `override` is almost always what you want.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 708.5 Td (**Senior:** "How does the CLR resolve virtual method calls?") Tj ET
0.94 0.96 0.99 rg 50 635.0 495 58 re f
0.15 0.45 0.85 rg 50 635.0 4 58 re f
0.85 0.88 0.92 RG 0.5 w 50 635.0 495 58 re s
BT /F2 8 Tf 0.15 0.45 0.85 rg 62 683.0 Td ([NOTE]) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 683.0 Td (      The CLR maintains a vtable per type. Each virtual method slot maps to a method address.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 672.0 Td (When a virtual call is made, CLR looks up the object's actual type, finds its vtable,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 661.0 Td (and dispatches to the address in the relevant slot. `override` replaces the slot; `new`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 650.0 Td (adds a new slot without replacing. This happens at IL level via `callvirt` instruction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 627.0 Td (**Tricky:** "Can you have polymorphism without inheritance?") Tj ET
0.94 0.96 0.99 rg 50 564.5 495 47 re f
0.15 0.45 0.85 rg 50 564.5 4 47 re f
0.85 0.88 0.92 RG 0.5 w 50 564.5 495 47 re s
BT /F2 8 Tf 0.15 0.45 0.85 rg 62 601.5 Td ([NOTE]) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 601.5 Td (      Yes - through interfaces. Interface-based polymorphism is preferred over class) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 590.5 Td (inheritance for behavioral substitution \(SOLID: DIP\). Also, C# generics with constraints) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.15 0.2 0.25 rg 62 579.5 Td (provide compile-time polymorphism. Duck typing \(via dynamic\) is another form.) Tj ET
