BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 790.0 Td (11. Real-World Production Problem: Kestrel Thread Pool Starvation) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 778.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 776.0 Td (**Incident**: An enterprise banking API experienced sudden 504 Gateway Timeouts under a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 765.0 Td (moderate spike of 3,000 concurrent users. CPU usage was under 25%, but request latency) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 754.0 Td (spiked from 30ms to 45,000ms.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 743.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 741.0 Td (**Root Cause**: A junior developer called a synchronous audit log client) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 730.0 Td (\(`auditLogger.LogAsync\(\).Wait\(\)`\) inside a custom middleware. Under load, all ThreadPool) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 719.0 Td (worker threads blocked waiting on tasks. New incoming TCP connections were accepted by) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 708.0 Td (Kestrel's socket listener but queued indefinitely in the ThreadPool queue.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 697.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 695.0 Td (**Diagnostic Procedure**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 682.0 Td (Run `dotnet-counters monitor -p <PID> System.Runtime`. Observed `ThreadPool Queue Length`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 670.5 Td (climbing to 12,000 while `ThreadPool Thread Count` spiked to its maximum limit.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 657.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 655.0 Td (**Resolution**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 642.0 Td (Replaced all `.Wait\(\)` and `.Result` invocations with `await`. Configured alerting on) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 630.5 Td (`ThreadPool.PendingWorkItemCount`.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 615.0 Td (12. Interview Preparation Master Questions) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 603.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 601.0 Td (**Basic:** *What is Kestrel and why does ASP.NET Core need it instead of IIS?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 588.0 Td (**Answer:** Kestrel is a lightweight, high-performance, cross-platform HTTP web server built) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 576.5 Td (into ASP.NET Core. In legacy .NET, applications were tightly coupled to Windows IIS via) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 565.0 Td (`System.Web.dll`. Kestrel decouples ASP.NET Core from Windows, enabling it to run on Linux,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 553.5 Td (Docker, and Kubernetes. IIS is now typically used only as a reverse proxy via the ASP.NET Core) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 542.0 Td (Module \(ANCM\).) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 528.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 526.5 Td (**Intermediate:** *What is the relationship between Kestrel, HttpContext, and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 515.5 Td (RequestDelegate?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 502.5 Td (**Answer:** Kestrel listens on network sockets, accepts raw TCP packets, parses them using) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 491.0 Td (`System.IO.Pipelines`, and packages the request data into an `IFeatureCollection`. It wraps this) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 479.5 Td (collection into a `DefaultHttpContext` and passes it to the application's compiled) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 468.0 Td (`RequestDelegate\(HttpContext\)` entry point.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 454.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 452.5 Td (**Senior:** *How does Kestrel prevent memory fragmentation and GC pauses when parsing) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 441.5 Td (millions of HTTP requests?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 428.5 Td (**Answer:** Kestrel uses `System.IO.Pipelines` backed by `ArrayPool<byte>.Shared` and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 417.0 Td (`MemoryPool<byte>`. Memory buffers are leased from the pool rather than allocated on the managed) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 405.5 Td (heap. The HTTP parser utilizes `ReadOnlySequence<byte>` and `Span<byte>` for zero-allocation) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 394.0 Td (slice parsing of headers and verbs, avoiding `string` allocations on the hot path.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 380.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 378.5 Td (**Tricky Question:** *If Kestrel is so fast, why do we still put Nginx, Envoy, or YARP in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 367.5 Td (front of it in enterprise production?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 354.5 Td (**Best Answer:** While Kestrel can be edge-facing, reverse proxies \(like Nginx, Envoy, AWS ALB,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 343.0 Td (or YARP\) provide vital perimeter capabilities: edge SSL/TLS offloading, DDoS filtering, IP) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 331.5 Td (whitelisting/geo-blocking, static file caching at scale, seamless multi-service path routing,) Tj ET
