BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 736.0 Td (**Basic:** *Why is structured logging preferred over traditional plain text logging in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 725.0 Td (ASP.NET Core?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 712.0 Td (**Answer:** Traditional logging formats messages as flat strings, making searching and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 700.5 Td (filtering difficult. Structured logging preserves the message template and keeps parameter names) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 689.0 Td (and values as distinct key-value pairs \(JSON properties\). This allows centralized logging tools) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 677.5 Td (\(like Elasticsearch, Splunk, Datadog\) to index and query specific fields \(e.g., `OrderId ==) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 666.0 Td ("123"` and `Amount > 500`\) with high performance.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 652.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 650.5 Td (**Intermediate:** *Why should you never use C# string interpolation \(`$""`\) when calling) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 639.5 Td (`ILogger` methods?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 626.5 Td (**Answer:** String interpolation executes immediately at the call site, allocating a new string) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 615.0 Td (on the managed heap and boxing any value-type parameters-even if the target log level is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 603.5 Td (disabled in configuration. Furthermore, it flattens the parameters into a single string,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 592.0 Td (destroying structured logging data properties and preventing log aggregators from indexing the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 580.5 Td (individual fields.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 567.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 565.0 Td (**Senior:** *How does `ILogger.BeginScope` work under the hood across asynchronous) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 554.0 Td (execution paths?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 541.0 Td (**Answer:** `ILogger.BeginScope` stores contextual state in an ambient asynchronous context) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 529.5 Td (powered by `System.Threading.AsyncLocal<T>`. When an asynchronous method yields \(`await`\), the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 518.0 Td (runtime preserves the execution context and flows the `AsyncLocal` state across thread) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 506.5 Td (transitions in the `ThreadPool`. Any log statement executed within the lexical or asynchronous) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 495.0 Td (lifetime of the scope automatically includes the scope's dictionary properties without needing) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 483.5 Td (to pass context objects through business methods.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 470.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 468.0 Td (**Tricky Question:** *How does ASP.NET Core support the W3C Trace Context standard for) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 457.0 Td (distributed tracing?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 444.0 Td (**Best Answer:** ASP.NET Core implements distributed tracing natively through) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 432.5 Td (`System.Diagnostics.Activity`. When an incoming HTTP request contains the standard `traceparent`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 421.0 Td (header \(`version-traceId-spanId-traceFlags`\), the runtime automatically parses it and assigns) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 409.5 Td (the `TraceId` to `Activity.Current`. If outgoing HTTP requests are made using `HttpClient`, the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 398.0 Td (framework automatically injects the corresponding `traceparent` header with the current) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 386.5 Td (`TraceId` and a newly generated child `SpanId`, enabling seamless end-to-end distributed tracing) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 375.0 Td (across microservices.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 359.5 Td (---) Tj ET
