BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 790.0 Td (**Answer:** Legacy Response Caching adhered strictly to client-driven HTTP `Cache-Control`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 778.5 Td (headers and lacked programmatic server-side control. .NET 8 Output Caching is a modern,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 767.0 Td (server-controlled caching architecture that supports: \(1\) **Cache Tags** enabling mass) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 755.5 Td (invalidation of related cached endpoints \(`EvictByTagAsync`\), \(2\) **Built-in Resource Locking**) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 744.0 Td (that prevents cache stampedes on the cached endpoint, \(3\) Custom eviction policies, and \(4\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 732.5 Td (Multiple storage backends including Redis via `IOutputCacheStore`.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 719.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 717.0 Td (**Tricky Question:** *Why does `IDistributedCache` only accept `byte[]` and `string`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 706.0 Td (instead of strongly-typed C# objects?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 693.0 Td (**Best Answer:** Because distributed caches reside out-of-process \(e.g., across a network in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 681.5 Td (Redis or Memcached\), objects cannot be shared by CLR memory reference. They must be serialized) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 670.0 Td (across the network boundary. ASP.NET Core leaves the serialization format intentionally) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 658.5 Td (unconstrained in `IDistributedCache` so developers can choose the optimal serializer for their) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 647.0 Td (throughput and CPU requirements \(e.g., `System.Text.Json`, `MessagePack`, or Google `Protobuf`\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 635.5 Td (rather than forcing a heavy, fixed serializer.) Tj ET
