BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 398.5 Td (**Why You Cannot Inject `DbContext` into a `BackgroundService` Constructor**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 385.5 Td (`BackgroundService` is registered as a **Singleton** in the DI container. `DbContext` is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 374.0 Td (registered as **Scoped** \(designed to live for the duration of an HTTP request or business) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 362.5 Td (transaction\). If you inject `DbContext` into the `BackgroundService` constructor, it creates a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 351.0 Td (**Captive Dependency**: the `DbContext` lives forever, caching entity state indefinitely,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 339.5 Td (growing memory without bound, and throwing concurrency exceptions if accessed across ticks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 328.0 Td (Injecting `IServiceScopeFactory` ensures each execution cycle gets a fresh, clean `DbContext`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 316.5 Td (that is properly disposed.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 303.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 301.0 Td (**Why `PeriodicTimer` Beats `System.Threading.Timer`**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 288.0 Td (`System.Threading.Timer` uses thread-pool callbacks that can overlap if execution time exceeds) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 276.5 Td (the interval, causing re-entrancy bugs. `PeriodicTimer` \(introduced in .NET 6\) is fully) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 265.0 Td (asynchronous, safe for `await`, avoids timer drift, and eliminates overlapping executions) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 253.5 Td (naturally.) Tj ET
