BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 619.5 Td (10. Common Mistakes) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 607.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 605.5 Td (**Calling gRPC without a Deadline**: Omitting deadlines on client calls. If the server) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 594.5 Td (hangs or deadlocks, the client waits forever, exhausting HTTP/2 streams and thread pools.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 583.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 581.5 Td (**Exposing gRPC Directly to Standard Browser SPAs**: Attempting to call gRPC from) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 570.5 Td (Angular/React without using **gRPC-Web** or an Envoy proxy.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 559.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 557.5 Td (**Altering Protobuf Field Numbers**: Modifying field index numbers \(`string account_id =) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 546.5 Td (1;` changed to `= 2;`\). In Protobuf, the binary wire format relies on the numeric tag, NOT) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 535.5 Td (the field name. Changing tags breaks backward/forward compatibility.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 522.5 Td (11. Real-World Production Problem: The Cascading Hang Disaster) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 510.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 508.5 Td (**Incident**: A payment processing cluster experienced a total outage across 8 services.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 497.5 Td (All container pods showed 100% thread pool exhaustion.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 486.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 484.5 Td (**Root Cause**: Service A called Service B via gRPC without a deadline) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 473.5 Td (\(`client.ProcessPaymentAsync\(request\)`\). Service B hung waiting on a deadlocked SQL row) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 462.5 Td (lock. Because no deadline was set, Service A's HTTP/2 connection held open, and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 451.5 Td (calling thread remained blocked. As incoming traffic continued, all threads across the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 440.5 Td (entire microservice chain were consumed waiting indefinitely.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 429.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 427.5 Td (**Resolution**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 414.5 Td (Enforced a mandatory deadline policy on all gRPC client invocations using a Polly timeout) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 403.0 Td (pipeline or `CallOptions.WithDeadline\(DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds\(3\)\)`.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 387.5 Td (12. Interview Preparation Master Questions) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 375.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 373.5 Td (**Basic:** *What is gRPC and what are its primary components?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 360.5 Td (**Answer:** gRPC is a high-performance, contract-first RPC framework. Its core components are:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 349.0 Td (\(1\) **HTTP/2** as the transport protocol \(supporting multiplexing and streaming\), \(2\) **Protocol) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 337.5 Td (Buffers \(Protobuf\)** as the Interface Definition Language \(IDL\) and binary serialization format,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 326.0 Td (and \(3\) Code-generated client and server stubs providing strongly-typed method invocations.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 312.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 310.5 Td (**Intermediate:** *What are the four communication modes supported by gRPC?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 297.5 Td (**Answer:**) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 284.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 282.0 Td (**Unary RPC**: Single request from client, single response from server \(traditional) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 271.0 Td (request/response\).) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 260.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 258.0 Td (**Server Streaming RPC**: Single request from client; server returns a stream of multiple) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 247.0 Td (response messages.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 236.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 234.0 Td (**Client Streaming RPC**: Client sends a stream of multiple messages; server responds) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 223.0 Td (with a single summary message.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 212.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 210.0 Td (**Bi-directional Streaming RPC**: Both client and server send a stream of messages) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 199.0 Td (concurrently using two independent streams over the same HTTP/2 connection.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 188.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 186.0 Td (**Senior:** *How does HTTP/2 multiplexing in gRPC overcome the Head-of-Line \(HoL\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 175.0 Td (blocking problem of HTTP/1.1 REST APIs?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 162.0 Td (**Answer:** In HTTP/1.1, a TCP connection can only handle one request/response exchange at a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 150.5 Td (time. If a request is slow, all subsequent requests on that connection must wait \(Head-of-Line) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 139.0 Td (blocking\), requiring clients to open multiple parallel TCP connections \(browsers open up to 6) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 127.5 Td (per domain\). HTTP/2 introduces binary framing and stream identifiers, allowing hundreds of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 116.0 Td (independent RPC requests and responses to be interleaved and multiplexed concurrently over a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 104.5 Td (**single persistent TCP connection**, maximizing throughput and eliminating application-layer) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 93.0 Td (HoL blocking.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 79.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 77.5 Td (**Tricky Question:** *How do you ensure backward and forward compatibility when updating) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 66.5 Td (a `.proto` file in a live enterprise production environment?*) Tj ET
