BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 790.0 Td (**Answer:** The standard HTTP status code is **`429 Too Many Requests`**. It should always be) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 778.5 Td (accompanied by the **`Retry-After`** response header, indicating how many seconds \(or the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 767.0 Td (specific date/time\) the client must wait before making another request.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 753.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 751.5 Td (**Intermediate:** *Explain the difference between a Fixed Window and a Sliding Window) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 740.5 Td (rate limiter.*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 727.5 Td (**Answer:** A Fixed Window limiter resets its request counter at fixed time intervals \(e.g.,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 716.0 Td (every 60 seconds\). It suffers from the **boundary burst problem**, where a client can send its) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 704.5 Td (maximum quota at the very end of Window 1 and again at the very beginning of Window 2, doubling) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 693.0 Td (the permitted traffic in a tiny timeframe. A Sliding Window limiter divides the window into) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 681.5 Td (smaller time segments and slides the window forward continuously, smoothing traffic and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 670.0 Td (preventing boundary burst spikes.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 656.5 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 654.5 Td (**Senior:** *How does the Token Bucket algorithm work, and why is it popular for) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 643.5 Td (enterprise REST APIs?*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 630.5 Td (**Answer:** The Token Bucket algorithm maintains a bucket with a maximum token capacity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 619.0 Td (Requests consume tokens. Tokens are continuously added back to the bucket at a constant refill) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 607.5 Td (rate. It is highly popular for REST APIs because it elegantly supports **burstiness**: a client) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 596.0 Td (that has been idle can consume its entire bucket capacity in a quick burst \(e.g., loading an) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 584.5 Td (initial dashboard\), while long-running traffic is strictly bounded by the steady-state) Tj ET
