BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 334.0 Td (**Unit of Work**: Maintains a list of business objects affected by a business transaction) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 323.0 Td (and coordinates the writing out of changes and the resolution of concurrency problems.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 312.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 310.0 Td (**Identity Map**: Ensures that each database row is loaded only once per context) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 299.0 Td (instance. If the entity is requested again, the Identity Map returns the reference to the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 288.0 Td (already loaded in-memory object rather than instantiating a duplicate.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 275.0 Td (2. Historical Reason) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 261.0 Td (In early .NET versions \(ADO.NET\), developers manually opened `SqlConnection`, orchestrated) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 249.5 Td (`SqlTransaction`, authored inline `SqlCommand` text, mapped `SqlDataReader` columns to object) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 238.0 Td (properties index by index, and manually synchronized state mutations.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 222.5 Td (When EF was introduced \(.NET Framework 3.5\), it utilized `ObjectContext` paired with EDMX XML) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 211.0 Td (schemas. `ObjectContext` was bloated, tightly coupled to COM-era entity data models, consumed) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 199.5 Td (excessive memory, and made unit testing difficult. EF Core completely stripped away the legacy) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.8 Tf 0.15 0.18 0.22 rg 50 188.0 Td (metadata layer, introducing a lightweight, decoupled, dependency-injection-native `DbContext`.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 172.5 Td (3. Problem It Solves) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 160.5 3 3 re f
