0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 465.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 463.0 Td (**Why POCO Snapshot over Proxy Objects?** EF6 generated heavy runtime Castle) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 452.0 Td (DynamicProxies that intercepted every getter/setter. This caused debugging nightmares,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 441.0 Td (serialization errors with JSON serializers \(which couldn't serialize dynamic proxy types\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 430.0 Td (and tight coupling. EF Core's snapshot tracking allows pure, clean domain models without) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 419.0 Td (proxy wrappers.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 408.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 406.0 Td (**Why Granular Column Updates?** If EF Core updated every column on every save, any) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 395.0 Td (concurrent update to an unrelated column on the same row would be silently overwritten) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 384.0 Td (with stale in-memory data. Granular updates minimize write conflict surface.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 371.0 Td (9. Performance Considerations) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 359.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 357.0 Td (**Memory Footprint:** Every tracked entity consumes double its scalar memory because EF) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 346.0 Td (Core stores both the active instance and the original snapshot values.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 335.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 333.0 Td (**`DetectChanges\(\)` CPU Spike:** If a context tracks 10,000 entities, calling `Add\(\)`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 322.0 Td (inside a loop triggers `DetectChanges\(\)` implicitly on each addition unless configured) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 311.0 Td (otherwise. This results in $O\(N^2\)$ algorithmic execution, locking the CPU core at 100%.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.35 0.45 0.55 rg 50 298.0 Td (10. Common Mistakes) Tj ET
