BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 406.0 Td (**Senior:** *How do `ExecuteUpdate` and `ExecuteDelete` work in EF Core 7+, and what are) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 395.0 Td (the trade-offs of using them?*) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 384.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 382.0 Td (**Answer:** They translate directly into single SQL `UPDATE` or `DELETE` statements) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 371.0 Td (executed directly on the database engine without materializing entities into memory or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 360.0 Td (utilizing the `ChangeTracker`. **Trade-offs:** They bypass EF Core lifecycle features,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 349.0 Td (including domain events, optimistic concurrency tokens, soft-delete global query filters,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 338.0 Td (and `SaveChangesInterceptor` audit trail logic.) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 327.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 325.0 Td (**Tricky Question:** *When should you use `EF.CompileAsyncQuery`? Does it speed up) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 314.0 Td (database execution?*) Tj ET
0.2 0.5 0.85 rg 53 303.0 3 3 re f
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 301.0 Td (**Answer:** It does **not** speed up database execution; the generated SQL and database) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 290.0 Td (query plan are identical. It speeds up *web server CPU processing* by pre-compiling the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 279.0 Td (expression tree into a callable delegate, bypassing the Query Compilation Cache hash) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 268.0 Td (lookup and AST visitor phases. It is strictly recommended for ultra-hot, high-frequency) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.18 0.2 0.25 rg 62 257.0 Td (microservice endpoints where microsecond-level C# execution optimizations matter.) Tj ET
